Task 1: Configure mTLS for Workloads
Subtask 1.1: Verify Current Istio Installation
First, let's verify that Istio is properly installed and check the current mTLS configuration.

# Check Istio installation status
kubectl get pods -n istio-system

# Verify Istio version
istioctl version

# Check current mesh configuration
kubectl get meshconfig -n istio-system -o yaml
Subtask 1.2: Deploy Sample Applications
Deploy the sample microservices that we'll use to demonstrate mTLS configuration.

# Create namespace for our applications
kubectl create namespace microservices

# Label the namespace for Istio injection
kubectl label namespace microservices istio-injection=enabled

# Deploy the sample applications
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: frontend
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: frontend
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: frontend
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: frontend
        image: nginx:1.21
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: frontend
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  selector:
    app: frontend
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: product-service
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: product-service
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: product-service
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: product-service
        image: httpd:2.4
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: product-service
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  selector:
    app: product-service
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
---
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: order-service
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: order-service
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: order-service
        version: v1
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: order-service
        image: httpd:2.4
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: order-service
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  selector:
    app: order-service
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 80
EOF
Subtask 1.3: Verify Pod Deployment and Sidecar Injection
Check that all pods are running and have Istio sidecars injected.

# Check pod status
kubectl get pods -n microservices

# Verify sidecar injection (should show 2/2 containers per pod)
kubectl get pods -n microservices -o wide

# Check sidecar proxy logs
kubectl logs -n microservices deployment/frontend -c istio-proxy --tail=10
Subtask 1.4: Configure Mesh-Wide mTLS Policy
Enable strict mTLS for the entire mesh by creating a mesh-wide policy.

# Create mesh-wide mTLS policy
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: istio-system
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
EOF
Subtask 1.5: Verify mTLS Configuration
Check that the mTLS policy has been applied correctly.

# Verify the peer authentication policy
kubectl get peerauthentication -n istio-system

# Check the policy details
kubectl describe peerauthentication default -n istio-system

# Verify mTLS status using istioctl
istioctl authn tls-check frontend.microservices.svc.cluster.local
Task 2: Set Up Peer Authentication Policies
Subtask 2.1: Create Namespace-Specific mTLS Policy
Configure mTLS policies at the namespace level for more granular control.

# Create namespace-specific peer authentication policy
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: microservices-mtls
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
EOF
Subtask 2.2: Configure Service-Specific mTLS Policy
Create a service-specific mTLS policy for the product service.

# Create service-specific peer authentication policy
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: product-service-mtls
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: product-service
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
EOF
Subtask 2.3: Configure Port-Specific mTLS Policy
Create a more granular policy that applies mTLS to specific ports.

# Create port-specific peer authentication policy
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: order-service-port-mtls
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: order-service
  portLevelMtls:
    80:
      mode: STRICT
EOF
Subtask 2.4: Create Destination Rules for mTLS
Configure destination rules to ensure proper mTLS communication.

# Create destination rules for mTLS
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: microservices-mtls
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  host: "*.microservices.svc.cluster.local"
  trafficPolicy:
    tls:
      mode: ISTIO_MUTUAL
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: product-service-mtls
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  host: product-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local
  trafficPolicy:
    tls:
      mode: ISTIO_MUTUAL
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata:
  name: order-service-mtls
  namespace: microservices
spec:
  host: order-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local
  trafficPolicy:
    tls:
      mode: ISTIO_MUTUAL
EOF
Subtask 2.5: Verify Peer Authentication Policies
Check that all policies are correctly applied and active.

# List all peer authentication policies
kubectl get peerauthentication -A

# Check destination rules
kubectl get destinationrule -n microservices

# Verify policy configuration
kubectl describe peerauthentication microservices-mtls -n microservices
Task 3: Verify Encrypted Communication Between Services
Subtask 3.1: Test Service-to-Service Communication
Create a test pod to verify that services can communicate with mTLS enabled.

# Create a test pod with curl
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: test-client
  namespace: microservices
  labels:
    app: test-client
spec:
  containers:
  - name: curl
    image: curlimages/curl:7.85.0
    command: ["/bin/sh"]
    args: ["-c", "sleep 3600"]
EOF
Subtask 3.2: Verify mTLS Communication
Test communication between services to ensure mTLS is working.

# Wait for test pod to be ready
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/test-client -n microservices --timeout=60s

# Test communication to product service
kubectl exec -n microservices test-client -- curl -s http://product-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local/

# Test communication to order service
kubectl exec -n microservices test-client -- curl -s http://order-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local/

# Test communication to frontend
kubectl exec -n microservices test-client -- curl -s http://frontend.microservices.svc.cluster.local/
Subtask 3.3: Analyze mTLS Traffic with istioctl
Use istioctl to analyze and verify mTLS traffic between services.

# Check mTLS status for all services
istioctl authn tls-check

# Check specific service mTLS status
istioctl authn tls-check product-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local

# Verify proxy configuration
istioctl proxy-config cluster test-client.microservices --fqdn product-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local
Subtask 3.4: Monitor mTLS Certificates
Examine the certificates being used for mTLS communication.

# Check certificate information
istioctl proxy-config secret test-client.microservices

# View detailed certificate information
kubectl exec -n microservices test-client -- openssl s_client -connect product-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local:80 -servername product-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local < /dev/null 2>/dev/null | openssl x509 -text -noout
Subtask 3.5: Test mTLS Enforcement
Verify that mTLS is properly enforced by testing with a non-mTLS client.

# Create a pod without Istio sidecar injection
kubectl create namespace test-no-istio

# Deploy test pod without sidecar
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: no-sidecar-client
  namespace: test-no-istio
spec:
  containers:
  - name: curl
    image: curlimages/curl:7.85.0
    command: ["/bin/sh"]
    args: ["-c", "sleep 3600"]
EOF
# Wait for pod to be ready
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/no-sidecar-client -n test-no-istio --timeout=60s

# Attempt to connect (should fail with strict mTLS)
kubectl exec -n test-no-istio no-sidecar-client -- curl -v --connect-timeout 5 http://product-service.microservices.svc.cluster.local/
Subtask 3.6: Monitor mTLS Metrics
Use Prometheus metrics to monitor mTLS usage and performance.

# Check mTLS-related metrics
kubectl exec -n microservices test-client -- curl -s http://localhost:15000/stats/prometheus | grep tls

# View connection security metrics
kubectl exec -n microservices deployment/frontend -c istio-proxy -- curl -s http://localhost:15000/stats/prometheus | grep -E "(tls|security)"
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Services Cannot Communicate After Enabling mTLS
Symptoms: Connection timeouts or refused connections between services.

Solution:

# Check if destination rules are properly configured
kubectl get destinationrule -n microservices -o yaml

# Verify peer authentication policies
kubectl get peerauthentication -A

# Check sidecar proxy logs
kubectl logs -n microservices deployment/frontend -c istio-proxy --tail=50
Issue 2: Certificate Validation Errors
Symptoms: TLS handshake failures or certificate validation errors.

Solution:

# Check certificate status
istioctl proxy-config secret test-client.microservices

# Verify certificate chain
kubectl exec -n microservices test-client -c istio-proxy -- openssl verify -CAfile /etc/ssl/certs/root-cert.pem /etc/ssl/certs/cert-chain.pem
Issue 3: Mixed mTLS Modes
Symptoms: Inconsistent behavior with some services working and others failing.

Solution:

# Check for conflicting policies
kubectl get peerauthentication -A -o yaml | grep -A 5 -B 5 mode

# Verify destination rule TLS modes
kubectl get destinationrule -A -o yaml | grep -A 5 -B 5 tls
Security Best Practices
Best Practice 1: Use Strict mTLS Mode
Always use STRICT mode in production environments to ensure all communication is encrypted.

# Example of strict mTLS policy
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: strict-mtls
  namespace: production
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
EOF
Best Practice 2: Implement Gradual mTLS Rollout
Use PERMISSIVE mode during migration phases to allow both mTLS and plain text traffic.

# Permissive mode for gradual rollout
cat <<EOF | kubectl apply -f -
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: migration-mtls
  namespace: staging
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: PERMISSIVE
EOF
Best Practice 3: Monitor Certificate Expiration
Regularly monitor certificate expiration and rotation.

# Check certificate expiration
istioctl proxy-config secret test-client.microservices -o json | jq '.dynamicActiveSecrets[0].secret.tlsCertificate.certificateChain.inlineBytes' | base64 -d | openssl x509 -text -noout | grep -A 2 "Not After"
